<script>
	import IndexAndTransaction from '../../diagrams/IndexAndTransaction.svelte';
</script>

## Why a query can be slow

When you ask the database for all the services of category 7, by default it does the simplest thing: it **reads the table row by row** and checks each one. This is called a **full scan** (in a query plan it is called `Seq Scan`, short for sequential scan). On a hundred rows you won’t notice it. On a million rows you will: the database reads a million rows to find ten.

## The index: the pointer at the back of a book

A good book has an alphabetical index at the back: “contract: 14, 87, 203”. You don’t leaf through the whole book for the word “contract”, you look it up in the index and go to the right pages. A database **index** works the same way: it is a separate ordered structure of column values and pointers to rows.

Most often it is a **tree**: on each level the values are laid out in order, and one step discards almost everything. A tree that branches hundreds of ways finds one row among millions in three or four steps. That is why a search through an index barely slows down when the table grows a thousand times.

Play with the diagram. The table-size slider and the “how many rows match” choice show how many pages the database reads with an index and without one. Try the smallest tables and the most common values.

<IndexAndTransaction />

What you should have noticed.

- **The gain grows with the table.** On a table of a hundred rows there is no difference. On a million rows with a rare value, the index reads hundreds of times less.
- **An index isn’t always needed.** If half the table matches (gender, yes/no, “active”), it is simpler to read it all: a trip through the index for every row costs more. The planner (the part of the database that chooses how to run a query) decides this itself.
- **Every index costs something.** An insert or an update has to change the table and every index. Five indexes mean five extra writes with every enquiry. They also take up space.

The rules that follow:

1. Build an index for a **specific, frequent query** that is slow, not “just in case” on every column.
2. A primary key and columns marked `UNIQUE` get an index automatically. But on **foreign keys** (`category_id`) PostgreSQL does not create an index by itself, and joins on them often get faster after you add one by hand.
3. An index can be **composite**: `ON items (category_id, day)` helps queries that filter by category (and by date as well). The order of the columns matters: such an index serves queries on `category_id` and on the pair, but not queries on `day` alone.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: is an index on a yes/no column worth building?</summary>

Almost never. The condition “active = true” matches about half the table, and it is simpler to read the table straight through than to make a trip through the index for every row.

</details>

## How to see what the database does: EXPLAIN

You don’t have to guess: before any query you can write the word `EXPLAIN`, and the database shows the **plan**, how it intends to run it.

```sql
EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM items WHERE category_id = 7;
```

In the plan of real PostgreSQL you will see `Seq Scan on items` (reads everything) or `Index Scan using … on items` (goes through the index), and also an estimate of the cost in arbitrary units. The `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` version also runs the query and measures the real time. In the practice below the sandbox prints a simplified model of a plan: how many rows it read. It is a teaching interpreter, not real PostgreSQL, and the numbers in its plan are different, but you can see the same difference between scanning the whole table and using an index.

## A transaction: all or nothing

Now the second topic, about crashes. Buying a ticket is **two writes**: take the seat off and record the order. If the server falls over between them, we get a seat taken with no order. The ticket is lost, and nobody will know. The two writes need to go through **together or not at all**.

That is what a **transaction** does: a group of commands that the database treats as one indivisible action.

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE seats SET free = free - 1;
INSERT INTO orders (name, phone) VALUES ('…', '…');
COMMIT;   -- everything is final now
```

`BEGIN` opens the transaction, `COMMIT` confirms all the changes at once, and `ROLLBACK` cancels them all. If the server falls over in the middle, everything before `COMMIT` is rolled back on its own. Look at the “Transaction” tab of the diagram: the mode without a transaction leaves the data in a broken state, and the mode with a transaction doesn’t.

The properties of transactions are gathered into the word **ACID**:

- **Atomicity**: all or nothing.
- **Consistency**: after a transaction the rules of the data hold, foreign keys lead where they should, and no constraint is broken.
- **Isolation**: two simultaneous transactions don’t see each other’s **unfinished** changes. This is what kept two editors from overwriting each other in the first lesson.
- **Durability**: what was confirmed with `COMMIT` won’t vanish, even if the power goes off right afterwards.

Isolation has **levels**: the stricter it is, the safer, and the more often transactions have to wait for each other. By default PostgreSQL chooses a moderate one (`READ COMMITTED`), and the strictest is called `SERIALIZABLE`. On your site the moderate one is almost always enough.

One last detail, but it saves you in shops and in booking appointments. Two buyers press “buy” for the last seat at the same moment. You can’t first **read** the number of seats and then **write** “that number minus one”: between those steps the second buyer reads the same number. It is safer in one statement: `UPDATE seats SET free = free - 1 WHERE free > 0`, and then check whether a row was changed. The condition and the change run indivisibly, and the second buyer is refused.

## Practice

You are in the era of 2022: sites now live on cloud databases, and designers think about load in advance. Take a table of two thousand services and speed up the query by category, then record an enquiry so that a crash can’t spoil it.

The page has three ready tables and five boxes. In the first, ask for the plan before the index; in the second, create an index and ask again (how many rows were read, write the numbers on the line under the box). Then in the third write a transaction of two inserts, in the fourth imitate a crash (no COMMIT), and in the fifth roll back and make sure there are no new enquiries.

## What was wrong with MySQL: no transactions

For many years the most popular table engine in MySQL was MyISAM: fast, simple, and **with no transactions and no foreign keys**. Thousands of PHP sites ran on it, and a crash between two writes left half-assembled orders in the database. The InnoDB engine, which has all of this, became the default only in MySQL 5.5, at the end of 2010. Today a database that can do transactions is taken for granted, and nobody wants to explain to a customer where their ticket went.
